thread:
sara-support: Customer on mobile app reporting 400 from POST /v1/rerank. Customer says they sent an `items` field but API returns `400 Invalid payload: items must be an array`. Need quick triage, cc @eli-engineer.
eli-engineer: Can you paste the exact raw request body + response body (no redaction necessary for schema bits)? Also provide the request-id from the logs if you have it.
sara-support: Pasting curl the customer sent and our proxied response below. Request-id: req_2026_0042

```bash
curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/rerank \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer sk_live_XXX" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "items": {
      "id": "conversation-123",
      "candidates": [
        {"text": "Option A", "score": "0.92"},
        {"text": "Option B", "score": "0.4"}
      ]
    },
    "top_k": 3
  }'
```

Response (proxied):

```json
HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
{
  "error": "Invalid payload: items must be a non-empty array of objects (received object)",
  "request_id": "req_2026_0042"
}
```

Note: customer library (third-party JSON builder) appears to turn single-item arrays into objects when only one item present. Also noticed they send candidate `score` as strings.

eli-engineer: Thanks. Two things to distinguish:
- client sent `items` as an object not an array (we should reject) -> server returned correct 400 but message could be clearer
- scores as strings: our schema expects numeric scores; currently we coerce if possible but should confirm behavior.
mike-support: Quick reply for customer: ask them to change to "\"items\": [ { ... } ]" and ensure `score` are numbers not strings. Example payload to paste:

```json
{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "conversation-123",
      "candidates": [
        {"text": "Option A", "score": 0.92},
        {"text": "Option B", "score": 0.4}
      ]
    }
  ],
  "top_k": 3
}
```

Also ask them to attach the exact SDK version that built the JSON.

anna-qa: Filing a quick unit test for the parser to cover: `items` as object -> 400 with path in error (e.g., `/items`), `score` as string -> either coerce or explicit 400. We should decide policy. My preference: be strict for types and return 400 with a field-level message so customers get actionable guidance.
